  "name": "Question 11",
  "problem": "Find the values of $x$ for which the function $g(x)=\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}-\\frac{7}{4}{x}^{2}+\\frac{3}{2}x+1$ is strictly increasing.\n\n$x<$\n{{field-1}} $\\,$ and $\\,x>$\n{{field-2}}",
  "worked_solution": "The function is strictly increasing where ${g}^{\u2032}(x)>0.$ Differentiating $g(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{\u2032}(x) & ={x}^{2}-\\frac{7}{2}x+\\frac{3}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we need to solve the inequality\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-\\frac{7}{2}x+\\frac{3}{2}>0.\n$$\n\nThe above inequality factors as\n\n$$\n(x-\\frac{1}{2})(x-3)>0.\n$$\n\nThe graph of $y=(x-\\frac{1}{2})(x-3)$ has roots at $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $x=3,$ as shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/assets/26374f7e5c86c5585d384b3e65b4a1b3fb2f94172ed753cdb3fe7bdb0af0abb8.png)\n\nFor an upward-opening parabola, the product $(x-\\frac{1}{2})(x-3)$ is positive when $x<\\frac{1}{2}$ or $x>3.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the function is strictly increasing on the intervals \n\n$$\nx<\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{and}\\,x>3.\n$$",
